title lasso_select -- given a query POLYGON, name every object it covers; concave lasso excludes what any convex query holds, GREEN
prompt baton prin recur -- pick up the running thread Lindy-first crux-first, Standfast on reds first, take the next agent-doable open-media rung and send it as its own signed round
think Grounded as Kyri: read KYRI.md and RADIANT_STYLE.md, then crux/REMEMBER.md (the live card) and crux/REDS.md (the ledger). Standfast check: every living REDS row is CLOSED or Released; only #75 rests as custody-gated errata (a deep-debride, a gate I do not cross). No agent-closable red open -- Standfast satisfied, new work licensed.
think The running thread (top three README rows + the newest recommend lines) is the Season G SCENE-READ family building a surface's interaction floor: scene_manifest enumerates, hit_test names the ONE object under a POINT, region_select names EVERY object a RECTANGLE covers. The card and the region_select recommend both name the crux-first next: a further scene read, and specifically a lasso / polygon select. That is the sharpest still-tractable rung -- one dimension the rectangle cannot express -- so Lindy-first crux-first takes it.
think Studied the compose surface before a byte: region_select.rye is the clean parent (prefilter/arbiter split, presence tally, ascending collect); fill_polygon.rye already proves the DOUBLED-coordinate even-odd device (sample each pixel at its odd half-integer center so no scanline lands on an even-coordinate vertex, the parity exact). contour.Point is the u32 vertex type. I wrote my OWN exact point-in-polygon rather than reuse fill_polygon's crossing_x (which @divFloor-rounds an intersection x) -- judged each crossing strictly-to-the-right by i128 CROSS-MULTIPLICATION, no division, no float, no tolerance: the family signature and the rung's owned arbiter.
think Designed the decisive fixture as a concave chevron (back (2,2), tip (8,6), back (2,10), notch (4,6)) with two objects: a tip object the loop covers and a notch object the concavity excludes -- proving a bounding box AND a convex hull can both hold an object a concave lasso carves out, the capability a marquee or any convex query can never express. Hand-verified point-in-poly for the tip and notch pixels before coding.
obs Metal caught a real geometry red before GREEN: my first notch bar sat at x=3, and the chevron's lower-left edge (2,10)->(4,6) slopes rightward, so at row 7 the loop boundary is at x~3.25 and the pixel center x=3.5 fell INSIDE the loop -- my by-symmetry assumption was wrong; the dart is asymmetric. Recomputed the excluded triangle (2,2)-(4,6)-(2,10) exactly, moved the bar to x=2 (center 2.5) where every row lands strictly inside it, GREEN on the second full run. Measurement beat memory; no red booked (caught pre-GREEN, in-round).
obs Compose not braid held: the rung owns only the point-in-polygon arbiter, the polygon bbox, and the box-overlap prefilter; label owns the at arbiter, scene_manifest the per-object rows, regions the bounding box, contour the Point vertex. A reading, not a render -- no plane of its own, no pixel written.
obs Every allocation and loop names a bound: 3..max_poly_vertices (4096) vertices, the candidate scan runs components times under label_max_pixels, the arbiter scan reads the polygon box clamped to the map under qoi's pixel ceiling with at most max_poly_vertices edges per pixel, the presence tally sized count+1. A degenerate (<3-vertex) polygon refuses DegeneratePoly having read nothing; an off-map lasso is a valid empty selection.
